<!-- AuthSeam — THE identity boundary. One file owns the session for the whole app.

     WHAT THIS REPLACES. Nine screens each carried `<api as="session" url="/dev-session.json">`
     and their own copy of an `authHeaders` computed, and three of them defined "signed out"
     as *that file 404ing* — so the app's own logged-out state was a network error and its
     only affordance was a "Reload session" button. There was no sign-in, no sign-out and no
     way to reach an account from another device. This file is the whole of that, once.

     THE CONTRACT (docs/auth.md). The server VERIFIES tokens; it never issues them. Everything
     here is one side of a documented seam:

       provider → { viewer: { sub, token, name?, email? }, operator?: { sub, token } }

     `sub` must be a UUID (owner RLS stores `owner_id uuid`), the token an HS256 JWS signed
     with DSX_JWT_SECRET. That is the ONLY thing the backend knows about identity.

     ══ THE SWAP POINT, IN ONE PLACE ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
     Four keys in App.json `consts` (a local build may override them in dsx.config.json
     `consts` without touching the shipped root plan):

       authSessionUrl   GET  → the payload above. Called at boot when a session is expected.
       authSignInUrl    POST { email, password } → the same payload.
       authSignUpUrl    POST { email, password } → the same payload.
       authSignOutUrl   POST → anything; the client drops its token either way.

     Set `authSignInUrl` and the credential form below becomes the sign-in surface. Leave it
     unset — this build's default — and the screen says so and offers the LOCAL provider
     (`scripts/dev-session.mjs`, served at /dev-session.json by the dev origin) instead of
     rendering a form that would accept any password. A control that promises something it
     does not do is the one thing this template never ships.

     WHY THE TOKEN IS NOT PERSISTED. Only the DECISION rides `dsx.cookie.sd_auth`
     ('in' | 'guest'); the token itself lives in `global.auth` — memory — and is re-fetched
     from the provider on every cold start. That is how a real IdP session works (the
     provider holds its own session; the app asks it for a short-lived access token), and it
     keeps a bearer JWT out of a JavaScript-readable cookie.

     HOW A SCREEN CONSUMES IT — three spellings, no session block, no authHeaders copy:

       <api as="wallet" url="/wallet/state" ssr="false"
            auto="global.auth.ok == true" headers="global.auth.headers"/>

     `auto=` is re-evaluated whenever its read set changes (kernel/src/api.ts —
     "refetch when the MATERIALIZED request changes"), so a block declared before this seam
     resolves simply waits, then fires the moment the token lands, and stops the moment it is
     dropped. `== true` and not truthiness: an unset global is NSNull, whose truthiness is
     not something to bet a money route on (AGENTS.md).

     GUEST BROWSING IS THE DEFAULT, on purpose. App Store 5.1.1(v): "If your app doesn't
     include significant account-based features, let people use it without a login." A drama
     catalogue does not need one to browse, so a first run is a GUEST: home, discover,
     browse, show pages and every free episode work with no account at all. Sign-in is asked
     for exactly where identity is — the wallet, an unlock, VIP, My List, comments.

     THE SURFACE IS REACHED FROM ANYWHERE by one push — `dsx.module.route.push({ path:
     '/auth/signin' })` (or `/auth/signout`, `/auth/delete`). One statement, so it fits an
     inline `on:tap`; no screen plumbs state into a child, and no screen has to own a modal.
     `<SignInCard>` is the card that does it. See "WHY THE SURFACE IS A ROUTE" in the head. -->
<vstack spacing="0" alignItems="stretch" on:appear="dsx.action.resolve()">
  <head>
    <!-- TWO MODES, ONE FILE. `boot` (the default) is the resolver: it publishes `global.auth`
         and renders NOTHING, and every screen mounts one. `page` is the surface, mounted
         exactly once by the /auth/:pane route (Components/Auth.dsx). Splitting them across
         two files would have split `publishIn` with them, which is the nine-copies disease
         in miniature — the whole point of this file. -->
    <attribute as="mode" default="'boot'"/>       <!-- boot | page -->
    <attribute as="pane" default="'signin'"/>     <!-- signin | signout | delete -->

    <!-- raised when the pane has finished its job, so the route screen can go back -->
    <event as="done" payload="what"/>

    <!-- THE PROVIDER CALLS. All four are imperative (`auto="false"`): the seam decides when
         identity is wanted, and a GET that fires on mount would re-authenticate on every
         route change. -->
    <api as="sessionCall" url="{{ dsx.variable.sessionUrl }}" auto="false" ssr="false"/>
    <api as="signInCall"  url="{{ dsx.variable.signInUrl }}"  method="POST" auto="false"/>
    <api as="signUpCall"  url="{{ dsx.variable.signUpUrl }}"  method="POST" auto="false"/>
    <api as="signOutCall" url="{{ dsx.variable.signOutUrl }}" method="POST" auto="false" headers="global.auth.headers"/>
    <api as="deleteCall"  url="{{ dsx.variable.deleteUrl }}"  method="POST" auto="false" headers="global.auth.headers"/>

    <!-- THE DEVICE-IDENTITY PROVIDER (server/auth.dsx; scripts/serve.mjs mints). These four are
         the 90% path: a device holds ONE server-generated credential and exchanges it for a
         short-lived session on every cold start, so favourites, history, the wallet and restore
         all work with no account. Imperative (`auto="false"`) — the seam decides when. -->
    <api as="deviceRegisterCall" url="{{ dsx.variable.deviceRegisterUrl }}" method="POST" auto="false"/>
    <api as="deviceExchangeCall" url="{{ dsx.variable.deviceUrl }}"         method="POST" auto="false"/>
    <api as="linkCall"   url="{{ dsx.variable.linkUrl }}"   method="POST" auto="false" headers="global.auth.headers"/>
    <api as="unlinkCall" url="{{ dsx.variable.unlinkUrl }}" method="POST" auto="false" headers="global.auth.headers"/>

    <variable as="email">return ''</variable>
    <variable as="password">return ''</variable>
    <variable as="mode">return 'in'</variable>        <!-- in | up -->
    <variable as="busy">return false</variable>
    <variable as="notice">return ''</variable>
    <variable as="confirmWord">return ''</variable>
    <variable as="deleteState">return ''</variable>   <!-- '' | done | absent | refused -->
    <variable as="deleteNote">return ''</variable>
    <!-- the device credential in hand this session, and where the vault says it lives. `plane`
         and `durability` drive the link-card copy (bound through global.auth); they are never
         authority — what a device OWNS is always a server answer to a server question. -->
    <variable as="devId">return ''</variable>
    <variable as="devSecret">return ''</variable>
    <variable as="devPlane">return 'none'</variable>
    <variable as="devDurability">return 'none'</variable>
    <!-- true when the vault answered an error that is NOT `not_found`: the store may HOLD a record
         it cannot show right now (a locked device, a missing keychain entitlement). Registering
         then would ORPHAN the device's data behind a new credential, so we do not — we wait. -->
    <variable as="vaultBlocked">return false</variable>
    <variable as="deviceBusy">return false</variable>

    <!-- THE CONFIG PLANE. `dsx.const.*` is App.json's `consts`, overlaid by dsx.config.json
         `consts` — flat scalars, reactive, and typed-NULL when absent, which is exactly the
         gate an honest degradation needs.

         ONE MEASURED CAVEAT, and it needs a guard rather than a note. The SSR renderer does
         NOT seed this plane — only the client bootloader does (`cli/src/build.ts` hands
         `consts` to `bootDsx`; `server/src/page-render.ts` seeds nothing) — so on a
         server-rendered first paint every key reads null. Unguarded, a cold load of
         /auth/signin on a deployment that HAS a provider would render "this build has no
         identity provider" and then flip. That is a wrong claim, which is worse than a late
         one, so both lanes wait on `constsKnown`: the whole plane is absent server-side and
         a dict on the client, which is a fact this component can actually test. Filed for
         PLAN.md §6 as the SSR-consts ask. -->
    <variable as="constsKnown" computed="true">return dsx.const != null</variable>
    <variable as="sessionUrl" computed="true">
      const u = dsx.const.authSessionUrl
      if (u == null || u == '') { return '/dev-session.json' }
      return u
    </variable>
    <variable as="signInUrl" computed="true">
      const u = dsx.const.authSignInUrl
      return u == null ? '' : u
    </variable>
    <variable as="signUpUrl" computed="true">
      const u = dsx.const.authSignUpUrl
      return u == null ? '' : u
    </variable>
    <variable as="signOutUrl" computed="true">
      const u = dsx.const.authSignOutUrl
      return u == null ? '' : u
    </variable>
    <variable as="deleteUrl" computed="true">
      const u = dsx.const.accountDeleteUrl
      return u == null ? '' : u
    </variable>
    <!-- THE DEVICE-PROVIDER ENDPOINTS, config-driven like every other seam URL. Defaults are the
         local provider (scripts/serve.mjs); an adopter points them at their own device-auth
         service exactly as they point authSignInUrl at their IdP. An EMPTY value turns the device
         lane off — the seam then falls back to the account/dev-session flow and the app is a plain
         guest, which is the honest degradation when no device provider is wired. -->
    <variable as="deviceRegisterUrl" computed="true">
      const u = dsx.const.authDeviceRegisterUrl
      if (u == null) { return '/auth/device/register' }
      return u
    </variable>
    <variable as="deviceUrl" computed="true">
      const u = dsx.const.authDeviceUrl
      if (u == null) { return '/auth/device' }
      return u
    </variable>
    <variable as="linkUrl" computed="true">
      const u = dsx.const.authLinkUrl
      if (u == null) { return '/auth/link' }
      return u
    </variable>
    <variable as="unlinkUrl" computed="true">
      const u = dsx.const.authUnlinkUrl
      if (u == null) { return '/auth/unlink' }
      return u
    </variable>
    <variable as="deviceEnabled" computed="true">return dsx.variable.deviceRegisterUrl != '' &amp;&amp; dsx.variable.deviceUrl != ''</variable>
    <!-- ONE test decides whether the credential form is real. No provider → no form. -->
    <variable as="hasProvider" computed="true">return dsx.variable.signInUrl != ''</variable>
    <variable as="canSignUp" computed="true">return dsx.variable.signUpUrl != ''</variable>
    <!-- ══ WHY THE SURFACE IS A ROUTE AND NOT A SHEET ═══════════════════════════════
         It was a sheet first, raised from any screen by writing one global, and that shape
         is broken in a way worth recording. A PUSHED route does not unmount the screen under
         it — a covered frame keeps its whole DOM, which is what makes the back gesture
         instant — and the router seeds a root frame under a DEEP-LINKED page so back works
         there too. So a cold load of /profile has two live frames, each with its own seam,
         each watching the same global, each portaling a sheet into the one app-wide overlay
         layer. Measured: two identical panels stacked at the same coordinates, two
         confirmation fields holding different text.

         Every ownership rule I could write from inside the component is wrong for one of
         those cases (a seeded under-frame mounts while the route ALREADY reads the deep
         link, so "the route I was born under" is the same string for both), and asking each
         caller to pass an owner id is the failure mode a template cannot afford.

         A ROUTE is exactly-once by construction, which is the framework's own guarantee
         rather than a protocol of mine. It costs nothing that mattered: a pushed frame keeps
         the screen underneath alive and restores it on pop, so the player never loses its
         place. And it buys something real — /auth/signin is a URL, so it survives a reload
         and can be the redirect target an OAuth provider hands back.

         (The template's other sheets are fine because they are driven by a LOCAL `open`
         variable: only the instance whose own trigger was tapped opens.) -->
    <variable as="isPage" computed="true">return dsx.attribute.mode == 'page'</variable>
    <variable as="submitLabel" computed="true">
      if (dsx.variable.busy) { return 'One moment…' }
      return dsx.variable.mode == 'up' ? 'Create account' : 'Sign in'
    </variable>
    <variable as="canDelete" computed="true">
      return dsx.variable.deleteUrl != '' && global.auth.ok == true
    </variable>
    <variable as="confirmed" computed="true">
      return dsx.variable.confirmWord == 'DELETE' && dsx.variable.busy == false
    </variable>

    <!-- ── the identity lifecycle. Nothing else in the app writes `global.auth` or
         `dsx.cookie.sd_auth`: every screen READS the published fact and pushes a route to
         change it. ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->

    <!-- Runs on every mount and does its work exactly once per app load: `state` is only
         absent before the first resolve, so a route change costs nothing. -->
    <action as="resolve">
      // ALREADY RESOLVED THIS APP LOAD. `state` is only null before the first resolve, so a route
      // change costs nothing but a re-arm of the refresh timer and a near-expiry check.
      if (global.auth != null && global.auth.state != null) {
        dsx.action.armRefresh()
        dsx.action.refreshIfStale()
        return
      }
      // AN ACCOUNT SESSION WINS. If the viewer signed in for real (the cookie says so), use the
      // existing provider flow — device identity is the DEFAULT, never a replacement for an account.
      if (dsx.cookie.sd_auth == 'in') {
        const r = await sessionCall.send()
        if (r.ok && r.data != null && r.data.viewer != null && r.data.viewer.token != null) {
          dsx.action.publishIn({ payload: r.data })
          return
        }
        // the account provider will not confirm — fall through to the device lane rather than
        // stranding the viewer as a bare guest with no storage at all
        dsx.cookie.sd_auth = 'guest'
      }
      // THE 90 PERCENT PATH. No account session, so become a DEVICE: read the credential from the
      // platform vault (web: the durable cookie), register one if there is none, exchange it for a
      // 1h session. Favourites, history, the wallet and restore then work with no account.
      await dsx.action.ensureDevice()
    </action>

    <!-- ── DEVICE IDENTITY (server/auth.dsx / scripts/serve.mjs). One credential in the vault,
         exchanged for a short-lived session; nothing here is ever a coin grant or an entitlement. -->

    <action as="ensureDevice">
      // ONE registration per boot: resolve() and a sign-out can both land here while the first
      // exchange is still in flight, and two registrations mint two devices (measured on the
      // web: two POST /auth/device/register per fresh session, the second overwriting the vault).
      if (dsx.variable.deviceBusy == true) { return }
      dsx.variable.deviceBusy = true
      await dsx.action.ensureDeviceNow()
      dsx.variable.deviceBusy = false
    </action>
    <action as="ensureDeviceNow">
      if (dsx.variable.deviceEnabled == false) {
        // no device provider wired on this deployment — the honest bare-guest state
        dsx.action.publishGuest()
        return
      }
      await dsx.action.readDurability()
      await dsx.action.readCred()
      if (dsx.variable.devId != '' && dsx.variable.devSecret != '') {
        const ex = await deviceExchangeCall.send({ deviceId: dsx.variable.devId, secret: dsx.variable.devSecret })
        if (ex.ok && ex.data != null && ex.data.session != null) {
          dsx.action.publishDevice({ payload: ex.data, deviceId: dsx.variable.devId })
          return
        }
        // the stored credential is no longer recognised (retired or unlinked elsewhere) — register anew
      } else if (dsx.variable.vaultBlocked) {
        // the vault refused the READ (locked device, missing entitlement) and there is no cookie
        // fallback — the store may hold a record it cannot show, so do NOT register over it. Browse
        // as a guest this boot; the next cold start (unlocked, or after the export fix) recovers it.
        dsx.action.publishGuest()
        return
      }
      const reg = await deviceRegisterCall.send({ platform: os, plane: dsx.variable.devPlane })
      if (reg.ok == false || reg.data == null || reg.data.session == null || reg.data.deviceId == null) {
        // the provider did not answer (offline, or none wired) — browse as a guest; the catalogue is public
        dsx.action.publishGuest()
        return
      }
      dsx.action.storeCred({ id: reg.data.deviceId, secret: reg.data.secret })
      dsx.action.publishDevice({ payload: reg.data, deviceId: reg.data.deviceId })
    </action>

    <!-- Read the credential: the vault first (iOS Keychain to iCloud, Android Block Store), then the
         cookie (the web has no cross-device credential plane, and the cookie is the native fallback
         until the export bundles the vault — PLAN 6.112). Writes to the seam variables, no envelope. -->
    <!-- ONE record, key `dsx.device`, value a JSON {deviceId, secret} — the IdentityVault audit recipe
         (dev@93a96b5f). `r.ok` → use the value; `r.error == 'not_found'` → there is nothing, register;
         ANY OTHER error (store_unavailable: locked device -25308, no entitlement -34018) → the store may
         HOLD a record it cannot show, so mark it BLOCKED and never mint over it — fall to the cookie. -->
    <action as="readCred">
      let blob = ''
      dsx.variable.vaultBlocked = false
      try {
        const r = await dsx.module.identityvault.read({ key: 'dsx.device' })
        if (r.ok && r.data != null && r.data.value != null) { blob = r.data.value }
        else if (r.error != null && r.error != 'not_found') { dsx.variable.vaultBlocked = true }
      } catch (e) {
        // the vault module is not in this build — the cookie is the whole story
      }
      // the cookie mirror: the web store (no cross-device plane) and the native fallback when the
      // Keychain / Block Store refused. Reading it here also clears `vaultBlocked` — we HAVE the record.
      if (blob == '' && dsx.cookie.dsx_device != null && dsx.cookie.dsx_device != '') {
        blob = dsx.cookie.dsx_device
        dsx.variable.vaultBlocked = false
      }
      dsx.variable.devId = ''
      dsx.variable.devSecret = ''
      if (blob != '' && blob != null) {
        try {
          const parsed = JSON.parse(blob)
          if (parsed != null && parsed.deviceId != null && parsed.secret != null) {
            dsx.variable.devId = parsed.deviceId
            dsx.variable.devSecret = parsed.secret
          }
        } catch (e) {
          // a corrupt record is no record — the caller registers a fresh one
        }
      }
    </action>

    <action as="storeCred" inputs="id, secret">
      dsx.variable.devId = id
      dsx.variable.devSecret = secret
      const blob = JSON.stringify({ deviceId: id, secret: secret })
      let vaultOk = false
      try {
        const w = await dsx.module.identityvault.write({ key: 'dsx.device', value: blob, locked: false })
        if (w.ok) { vaultOk = true }
      } catch (e) {
        // no vault in this build; the cookie below carries it
      }
      // THE COOKIE MIRROR, wherever the vault did not durably keep it — keyed on the vault RESULT and
      // the durability plane, never on `os`: a web tab (durability 'session'), a store that refused
      // the write (a simulator with no keychain entitlement), or a build with no vault. On a native
      // device that DID keep it (durability 'device'/'synced'), the secret stays in the secure store
      // and never touches a JS-readable cookie. 400 days is the platform cap the fold clamps to.
      if (vaultOk == false || dsx.variable.devDurability == 'session' || dsx.variable.devDurability == 'none') {
        dsx.cookie.dsx_device = { value: blob, days: 400 }
      }
    </action>

    <action as="clearCred">
      dsx.variable.devId = ''
      dsx.variable.devSecret = ''
      try {
        await dsx.module.identityvault.write({ key: 'dsx.device', value: '', locked: false })
      } catch (e) {
        // nothing to clear in a build without the vault
      }
      dsx.cookie.dsx_device = null
    </action>

    <!-- Ask the vault what it will actually do; the plane it reports IS the truth on every lane
         (indexeddb on the web, keychain on iOS, blockstore on Android). Keyed on the vault answering,
         never on `os` — the package is folded into every export now, so a platform guess would be a
         guess where a real fact is available. When it cannot answer, the pessimistic 'none' is what
         urges a link. -->
    <action as="readDurability">
      try {
        const d = await dsx.module.identityvault.durability()
        if (d.ok && d.data != null && d.data.plane != null) {
          dsx.variable.devPlane = d.data.plane
          dsx.variable.devDurability = d.data.durability == null ? 'none' : d.data.durability
          return
        }
      } catch (e) {
        // the vault is not in this build, or would not answer — fall through to the pessimistic none
      }
      dsx.variable.devPlane = 'none'
      dsx.variable.devDurability = 'none'
    </action>

    <action as="publishDevice" inputs="payload, deviceId">
      const h = { authorization: 'Bearer ' + payload.session }
      const ttl = payload.expiresIn == null ? 3600 : payload.expiresIn
      // A LINKED device exchanges to kind 'account' (its rows already merged into the account); an
      // unlinked device is kind 'device'. `deviceId` travels either way, so a sign-out on a linked
      // device knows to UNLINK rather than call an account provider it never used.
      global.auth = {
        state: 'in',
        ok: true,
        kind: payload.kind == null ? 'device' : payload.kind,
        sub: payload.sub == null ? '' : payload.sub,
        name: '',
        email: '',
        deviceId: deviceId == null ? '' : deviceId,
        headers: h,
        opHeaders: h,
        expiresAt: Date.now() + ttl * 1000,
        plane: dsx.variable.devPlane,
        durability: dsx.variable.devDurability
      }
      // NOT the account cookie: a real sign-in is still the only thing that flips sd_auth to 'in',
      // and a reload re-derives the device session from the vault rather than from a stored token.
      dsx.action.armRefresh()
    </action>

    <!-- ONE keyed timer app-wide (jse timers: a same-key setTimeout REPLACES rather than stacks), so
         a route change re-arms it instead of piling up. Re-exchange the credential ~2 minutes before
         the session expires; the device lane only. No busy loop. -->
    <action as="armRefresh">
      if (global.auth == null) { return }
      if (global.auth.deviceId == null || global.auth.deviceId == '') { return }
      if (global.auth.expiresAt == null) { return }
      let ms = global.auth.expiresAt - Date.now() - 120000
      if (ms < 30000) { ms = 30000 }
      setTimeout(() => { dsx.action.refreshDevice() }, ms, 'dsx.authRefresh')
    </action>

    <action as="refreshIfStale">
      if (global.auth == null || global.auth.expiresAt == null) { return }
      if (global.auth.deviceId == null || global.auth.deviceId == '') { return }
      // within 5 minutes of expiry, re-exchange now rather than waiting on the timer
      if (global.auth.expiresAt - Date.now() < 300000) { dsx.action.refreshDevice() }
    </action>

    <action as="refreshDevice">
      // the raw credential lives in the vault, not in this per-mount instance, so re-read it first
      await dsx.action.readCred()
      if (dsx.variable.devId == '' || dsx.variable.devSecret == '') { return }
      const ex = await deviceExchangeCall.send({ deviceId: dsx.variable.devId, secret: dsx.variable.devSecret })
      if (ex.ok && ex.data != null && ex.data.session != null) {
        dsx.action.publishDevice({ payload: ex.data, deviceId: dsx.variable.devId })
      }
    </action>

    <!-- After a REAL sign-in, link this device so the list, coins and history the viewer built as a
         guest merge into the account. Best-effort: a failed link never blocks the sign-in (the
         account session is already good and the data is still on the device to retry). On success the
         local credential is dropped, so a later sign-out drops to a FRESH device, not the merged one. -->
    <action as="linkAfterSignIn">
      await dsx.action.readCred()
      if (dsx.variable.devId == '' || dsx.variable.devSecret == '') { return }
      const r = await linkCall.send({ deviceId: dsx.variable.devId, secret: dsx.variable.devSecret })
      if (r.ok) { dsx.action.clearCred() }
    </action>

    <!-- ARGUMENTS TO A DECLARED ACTION ARE A NAMED DICT, not positional values, and this
         is worth the sentence because the failure is silent: `dsx.action.publishIn(r.data)`
         passes a dict whose keys are not the declared input names, so `payload` falls back
         to a caller-scope reading of the bare word (the ROW scope, which has no such key)
         and resolves NULL. Measured here: the seam published `Bearer ` with an empty token
         and every authenticated read answered 401 while `global.auth.ok` said true. The
         runner is explicit about it — callAction does `Object.assign(scope, callArgs)`. -->
    <action as="publishIn" inputs="payload">
      const v = payload.viewer
      const h = { authorization: 'Bearer ' + v.token }
      // THE OPERATOR IS THE SAME SEAM. Locally the dev minter hands back a second identity
      // carrying the service-role claim (the writes on the Manage surface need it). The
      // literal spelling of that claim is deliberately not in this file: scripts/dist-guard.mjs
      // refuses any build artefact containing it, and the guard is right to.
      // In production there is no second token: the operator is whoever the IdP marks with
      // that claim, so the viewer headers ARE the operator headers, and the gateway of the
      // host (`reach=""`) decides. Either way the Manage screen reads one name.
      const op = payload.operator == null ? h : { authorization: 'Bearer ' + payload.operator.token }
      global.auth = {
        state: 'in',
        ok: true,
        kind: 'account',
        sub: v.sub == null ? '' : v.sub,
        name: v.name == null ? '' : v.name,
        email: v.email == null ? '' : v.email,
        deviceId: '',
        headers: h,
        opHeaders: op,
        expiresAt: null,
        plane: 'none',
        durability: 'none'
      }
      dsx.cookie.sd_auth = 'in'
    </action>

    <action as="publishGuest">
      // headers is an EMPTY DICT, never null and never a null-valued key: a header whose
      // value is null is a doc-11 "missing-value" hole that gates the block forever.
      // `kind` is '' for a guest — data features read `ok`, account-only ones read `kind == 'account'`.
      global.auth = {
        state: 'guest', ok: false, kind: '', sub: '', name: '', email: '', deviceId: '',
        headers: {}, opHeaders: {}, expiresAt: null,
        plane: dsx.variable.devPlane, durability: dsx.variable.devDurability
      }
    </action>

    <!-- THE VIEWER-SCOPED CACHES DIE WITH THE VIEWER. docs/auth.md names these five: they
         are one person's coin balance, list, history, ledger and rewards state, kept
         app-wide so a tab revisit paints stale-then-fresh instead of blank (PLAN.md §6.30).
         Whatever drops the token must null them in the same breath or the next viewer sees
         the previous one's money for a frame. The CONTENT keys (cacheHome, cacheDiscover,
         cacheCatalog, cacheShow, cacheInbox) are public and stay. -->
    <action as="clearViewerCaches">
      global.cacheWallet = null
      global.cacheFavs = null
      global.cacheContinue = null
      global.cacheLedger = null
      global.cacheRewards = null
    </action>

    <!-- THE ORDER OF THESE FOUR LINES IS LOAD-BEARING, and it was wrong first: measured on
         a signed-in Account screen, the wallet stash came BACK two seconds after sign-out.
         A cookie write calls invalidateCookies(), which is part of every GET block's
         materialized identity — so writing the cookie FIRST made every authenticated block
         refetch WITH THE TOKEN STILL LIVE, and those responses landed after the caches were
         cleared and re-published the previous viewer money into the app-wide store.
         Drop the identity first (`auto` goes false, so nothing new can fire), then clear,
         and only then touch the cookie. The `on:success` writes are guarded as well — one
         request already in flight when the viewer taps Sign out cannot be reordered away. -->
    <action as="signOut">
      // A LINKED DEVICE signs out by UNLINKING — the credential is retired and the device starts
      // fresh, while the account keeps everything the merge already moved to it. A REAL account
      // signs out through its provider. Either way the viewer drops to a DEVICE identity, never to a
      // bare guest, because device auth is the baseline this app is built on (the founder rule).
      if (global.auth != null && global.auth.deviceId != null && global.auth.deviceId != '') {
        await unlinkCall.send({ deviceId: global.auth.deviceId })
        dsx.action.clearCred()
      } else if (dsx.variable.signOutUrl != '') {
        await signOutCall.send({})
      }
      // ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING (docs/auth.md): drop the identity first so `auto` goes false and no
      // authenticated block can refetch, THEN clear the caches, THEN touch the cookie.
      dsx.action.publishGuest()
      dsx.action.clearViewerCaches()
      dsx.cookie.sd_auth = 'guest'
      // re-establish a device identity so the viewer keeps a working (empty) session to browse and save under
      await dsx.action.ensureDevice()
      dsx.action.finish({ what: 'signedout' })
    </action>

    <!-- the pane is finished: clear anything typed and tell the route screen to go back -->
    <action as="finish" inputs="what">
      dsx.variable.password = ''
      dsx.variable.notice = ''
      dsx.variable.confirmWord = ''
      dsx.event('done', { what: what })
    </action>

    <action as="setMode" inputs="next">
      dsx.variable.mode = next
      dsx.variable.notice = ''
    </action>

    <!-- The credential lane. Only reachable when a provider is configured. -->
    <action as="submit">
      if (dsx.variable.busy) { return }
      const em = dsx.variable.email
      const pw = dsx.variable.password
      if (em == '' || pw == '') {
        dsx.variable.notice = 'Enter your email and your password.'
        return
      }
      dsx.variable.busy = true
      dsx.variable.notice = ''
      // `await` is a STATEMENT, never a ternary branch — the ternary form returns something
      // whose .ok is falsy, silently (AGENTS.md / PLAN.md §6.31).
      let r = null
      if (dsx.variable.mode == 'up') { r = await signUpCall.send({ email: em, password: pw }) }
      else { r = await signInCall.send({ email: em, password: pw }) }
      dsx.variable.busy = false
      if (r.ok && r.data != null && r.data.viewer != null && r.data.viewer.token != null) {
        dsx.action.publishIn({ payload: r.data })
        // link the guest device (if any) so its list, coins and history merge into the account
        await dsx.action.linkAfterSignIn()
        dsx.action.finish({ what: 'signedin' })
        return
      }
      // A declared rejection reaches `api` as { ok:false, status, data:{reason,message} };
      // `r.error` is transport only (timeout/network/parse). Show the words the server chose.
      if (r.data != null && r.data.message != null) { dsx.variable.notice = r.data.message }
      else if (dsx.variable.mode == 'up') { dsx.variable.notice = 'That account could not be created.' }
      else { dsx.variable.notice = 'That email and password did not match an account.' }
    </action>

    <!-- The LOCAL lane. `scripts/dev-session.mjs` mints one demo viewer and the dev origin
         serves it to private-network hosts only. There are no credentials to check, which
         is why this is a button and not a login form. -->
    <action as="useLocal">
      if (dsx.variable.busy) { return }
      dsx.variable.busy = true
      dsx.variable.notice = ''
      const r = await sessionCall.send()
      dsx.variable.busy = false
      if (r.ok && r.data != null && r.data.viewer != null && r.data.viewer.token != null) {
        dsx.action.publishIn({ payload: r.data })
        // link the guest device (if any) so its list, coins and history merge into the account
        await dsx.action.linkAfterSignIn()
        dsx.action.finish({ what: 'signedin' })
        return
      }
      dsx.variable.notice = 'The local session provider did not answer. Run `npm run session`, restart the origin, and try again.'
    </action>

    <!-- ACCOUNT DELETION — App Store 5.1.1(v): the app must offer to delete the entire
         account record and its personal data, in-app, with no support ticket in the way.
         The regulated-industry carve-out (5.1.1(ix)) covers banking, healthcare, gambling,
         cannabis, air travel and crypto; a drama app is none of them.

         This is the CLIENT half. The erase itself has to be one server action, because the
         rows live behind owner-scoped RLS across ten entities and there is no client route
         that can touch nine of them. `accountDeleteUrl` names it; when it is unset the pane
         below says exactly what is missing instead of showing a button that cannot work. -->
    <action as="deleteAccount">
      if (dsx.variable.confirmed == false) { return }
      dsx.variable.busy = true
      dsx.variable.deleteNote = ''
      // THE SWEEP LOOPS. A request gets 64 module calls, so /viewer/delete clears a bounded
      // batch and answers `done: false` while rows remain — a single call would leave a large
      // account HALF DELETED while reporting success, which is precisely the "deactivate"
      // outcome 5.1.1(v) refuses. Bounded at 60 passes to match the ceiling on that route; if
      // it is still not done by then the account is not empty and this must NOT claim it is.
      // (No apostrophes in an action-body comment: the balance scanner counts them as quotes.)
      // THE SUBJECT TRAVELS WITH THE REQUEST, and only as a LIST FILTER. Two of the tables a
      // deletion has to empty are ownership="public-read" (comment, report), so RLS does not
      // narrow a read there and the sweep has to name the caller rows itself — through the
      // declared owner columns, which a filter can address and `owner_id` cannot. Sending it
      // grants nothing: every delete underneath still runs as this token, so a forged subject
      // enumerates rows it cannot remove. The server answers `scoped: false` if it arrives
      // empty, and this loop treats that as not-done rather than as success.
      const who = global.auth.sub
      let r = await deleteCall.send({ confirm: 'DELETE', sub: who })
      let passes = 1
      while (r.ok && r.data != null && r.data.done == false && passes < 60) {
        r = await deleteCall.send({ confirm: 'DELETE', sub: who })
        passes = passes + 1
      }
      dsx.variable.busy = false
      if (r.ok && r.data != null && r.data.done == false) {
        dsx.variable.deleteState = 'refused'
        dsx.variable.deleteNote = 'Some of your data could not be removed. Nothing has been signed out — try again, and contact support if it repeats.'
        return
      }
      if (r.ok) {
        // same order as signOut, for the same measured reason
        dsx.action.publishGuest()
        dsx.action.clearViewerCaches()
        dsx.cookie.sd_auth = 'guest'
        dsx.variable.deleteState = 'done'
        dsx.variable.confirmWord = ''
        return
      }
      // 404 from the router of the host is `unknown_route` — the deployment has not declared the
      // action. Name it; do not report it as "something went wrong".
      const reason = r.data == null || r.data.reason == null ? '' : r.data.reason
      if (r.status == 404 || reason == 'unknown_route') {
        dsx.variable.deleteState = 'absent'
        dsx.variable.deleteNote = ''
        return
      }
      dsx.variable.deleteState = 'refused'
      dsx.variable.deleteNote = r.data != null && r.data.message != null
        ? r.data.message
        : 'The deletion could not be completed. Nothing was removed.'
    </action>

    <style as="body" spacing="16" alignItems="stretch" paddingH="20" paddingTop="8" paddingBottom="26"/>
    <style as="head" alignItems="center" spacing="12"/>
    <style as="title" fontSize="20" fontWeight="bold" color="#FFFFFF" tracking="-0.3"/>
    <style as="sub" fontSize="13" color="rgba(255,255,255,0.55)" lineSpacing="3"/>
    <style as="closeBtn" width="32" height="32" radius="16" background="rgba(255,255,255,0.1)" alignItems="center"/>

    <style as="field" background="#1C1C22" radius="12" paddingH="14" paddingV="12" spacing="10" alignItems="center"/>
    <style as="fieldCap" fontSize="12" color="rgba(255,255,255,0.45)"/>
    <style as="primary" background="#FF2C55" radius="22" paddingV="14" fontSize="15" fontWeight="semibold" color="#FFFFFF"/>
    <style as="danger" background="#8E1637" radius="22" paddingV="14" fontSize="15" fontWeight="semibold" color="#FFFFFF"/>
    <style as="ghost" background="rgba(255,255,255,0.08)" radius="22" paddingV="13" fontSize="14" fontWeight="semibold" color="#FFFFFF"/>
    <style as="switcher" spacing="8" alignItems="center" paddingTop="2"/>
    <style as="switchLink" fontSize="13" fontWeight="semibold" color="#FF6B8A"/>

    <style as="noticeRow" background="rgba(246,182,61,0.12)" radius="10" paddingH="12" paddingV="10" spacing="10" alignItems="flex-start"/>
    <style as="noticeLine" fontSize="13" fontWeight="medium" color="#F6B63D" lineSpacing="3"/>
    <style as="bridge" background="#1C1C22" radius="12" paddingH="14" paddingV="14" spacing="8" alignItems="stretch"/>
    <style as="bridgeTitle" fontSize="14" fontWeight="semibold" color="#FFFFFF"/>
    <style as="bridgeBody" fontSize="13" color="rgba(255,255,255,0.6)" lineSpacing="4"/>
    <style as="legal" fontSize="12" color="rgba(255,255,255,0.42)" lineSpacing="4"/>

    <style as="dangerCard" background="rgba(142,22,55,0.18)" radius="12" paddingH="14" paddingV="14" spacing="8" alignItems="stretch"/>
    <style as="killList" spacing="7" alignItems="stretch" paddingTop="2"/>
    <style as="killRow" spacing="10" alignItems="flex-start"/>
    <style as="killTxt" fontSize="13" color="rgba(255,255,255,0.75)" lineSpacing="3"/>
    <style as="doneCard" background="#141419" radius="12" paddingH="14" paddingV="18" spacing="10" alignItems="center"/>
  </head>

  <!-- SELF-SUFFICIENT: this part calls the shared design vocabulary (Components/parts/
       Theme.dsx), and a caller must not have to remember to register it first. The
       block is app-wide and last-write-wins, so a second mount costs a 0x0 stack. -->
  <Theme/>
  <!-- THE SURFACE. Nothing renders in `boot` mode: a screen mounting the resolver gets a
       zero-height stack and no chrome. The /auth/:pane route mounts this once with
       mode="page" and the pane it was asked for. -->
  <vstack spacing="0" alignItems="stretch" visible-if="dsx.variable.isPage">

    <!-- ══ PANE 1 — SIGN IN / CREATE ACCOUNT ═════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <vstack class="body" visible-if="dsx.attribute.pane != 'signout' &amp;&amp; dsx.attribute.pane != 'delete'">
      <hstack class="head">
        <vstack spacing="4" style="flex: 1; min-width: 0" alignItems="stretch">
          <text value="{{ dsx.variable.mode == 'up' ? 'Create your account' : 'Sign in' }}" class="title"/>
          <text class="sub" value="Coins, VIP, your list and your history belong to an account — so they follow you to any device. Browsing stays open either way."/>
        </vstack>
      </hstack>

      <hstack class="noticeRow" visible-if="dsx.variable.notice != ''">
        <image icon="exclamationmark.triangle.fill" iconSize="14" color="{{ gold() }}" a11yHidden="true"/>
        <text value="{{ dsx.variable.notice }}" class="noticeLine" style="flex: 1; min-width: 0"/>
      </hstack>

      <!-- the credential form: rendered only when a provider is actually behind it -->
      <vstack spacing="12" alignItems="stretch" visible-if="dsx.variable.constsKnown &amp;&amp; dsx.variable.hasProvider">
        <vstack spacing="6" alignItems="stretch">
          <text value="Email" class="fieldCap"/>
          <hstack class="field">
            <image icon="person.crop.circle" iconSize="16" color="rgba(255,255,255,0.4)" a11yHidden="true"/>
            <textfield bind="dsx.variable.email" placeholder="you@example.com" color="#FFFFFF"
                       style="flex: 1" a11yLabel="Email address"/>
          </hstack>
        </vstack>
        <vstack spacing="6" alignItems="stretch">
          <text value="Password" class="fieldCap"/>
          <hstack class="field">
            <image icon="lock.fill" iconSize="16" color="rgba(255,255,255,0.4)" a11yHidden="true"/>
            <textfield bind="dsx.variable.password" placeholder="Your password" secure="true" color="#FFFFFF"
                       style="flex: 1" a11yLabel="Password" on:submit="dsx.action.submit()"/>
          </hstack>
        </vstack>
        <button label="{{ dsx.variable.submitLabel }}" class="primary" on:tap="dsx.action.submit()"/>
        <hstack class="switcher" visible-if="dsx.variable.canSignUp">
          <text value="{{ dsx.variable.mode == 'up' ? 'Already have an account?' : 'New here?' }}" class="legal"/>
          <text class="switchLink" a11yTrait="button"
                value="{{ dsx.variable.mode == 'up' ? 'Sign in' : 'Create an account' }}"
                on:tap="dsx.action.setMode({ next: dsx.variable.mode == 'up' ? 'in' : 'up' })"/>
        </hstack>
      </vstack>

      <!-- NO PROVIDER: the gap, named, with the local implementation offered in its place.
           An email/password form in front of a minter that checks neither would be theatre
           — and this template deletes controls that promise what they cannot do. -->
      <vstack class="bridge" visible-if="dsx.variable.constsKnown &amp;&amp; dsx.variable.hasProvider == false">
        <text value="This build has no identity provider" class="bridgeTitle"/>
        <text class="bridgeBody" value="Locally the session comes from `scripts/dev-session.mjs` — one demo viewer, minted with the same HS256 token the server verifies in production. There are no credentials to check, so there is no login form here. Set `authSignInUrl` (and `authSignUpUrl`) in App.json `consts` and this screen becomes the real one: same payload, same token contract. docs/auth.md has the shape."/>
        <button label="{{ dsx.variable.busy ? 'One moment…' : 'Continue as the demo viewer' }}"
                class="primary" on:tap="dsx.action.useLocal()"/>
      </vstack>

      <button label="Keep browsing as a guest" class="ghost" on:tap="dsx.action.finish({ what: 'cancel' })"/>

      <!-- Terms and Privacy belong on the surface where an account is created (App Store
           3.1.2 / Play's policy centre want them reachable in-app). <LegalLinks> names the
           gap when a deployment has not set them, rather than shipping a dead link. -->
      <LegalLinks variant="line"/>
    </vstack>

    <!-- ══ PANE 2 — SIGN OUT ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         A confirm, because signing out on a shared device is destructive to the session and
         a mis-tap on a settings row should not cost a viewer their place. It also states
         what survives: nothing is deleted, and everything is waiting on the next sign-in. -->
    <vstack class="body" visible-if="dsx.attribute.pane == 'signout'">
      <hstack class="head">
        <vstack spacing="4" style="flex: 1; min-width: 0" alignItems="stretch">
          <text value="Sign out?" class="title"/>
          <text class="sub"
                value="Your coins, VIP pass, list and history stay on the account — they are all here again the moment you sign back in. The catalogue keeps working while you are out."/>
        </vstack>
      </hstack>
      <button label="Sign out" class="primary" on:tap="dsx.action.signOut()"/>
      <button label="Stay signed in" class="ghost" on:tap="dsx.action.finish({ what: 'cancel' })"/>
    </vstack>

    <!-- ══ PANE 3 — DELETE ACCOUNT ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <vstack class="body" visible-if="dsx.attribute.pane == 'delete'">
      <hstack class="head">
        <vstack spacing="4" style="flex: 1; min-width: 0" alignItems="stretch">
          <text value="Delete your account" class="title"/>
          <text value="This cannot be undone." class="sub"/>
        </vstack>
      </hstack>

      <!-- done -->
      <vstack class="doneCard" visible-if="dsx.variable.deleteState == 'done'">
        <image icon="checkmark.circle.fill" iconSize="30" color="{{ gold() }}" a11yHidden="true"/>
        <text value="Your account is deleted" fontSize="15" fontWeight="semibold" color="#FFFFFF"/>
        <text class="bridgeBody" textAlign="center"
              value="Everything held against it is gone and you are signed out. The catalogue stays open — you can keep browsing as a guest, or create a new account whenever you like."/>
        <button label="Done" class="ghost" on:tap="dsx.action.finish({ what: 'deleted' })"/>
      </vstack>

      <vstack spacing="16" alignItems="stretch" visible-if="dsx.variable.deleteState != 'done'">
        <vstack class="dangerCard">
          <text value="What is deleted" class="bridgeTitle"/>
          <vstack class="killList">
            <hstack class="killRow">
              <image icon="minus" iconSize="12" color="rgba(255,255,255,0.4)" a11yHidden="true"/>
              <text class="killTxt" style="flex: 1; min-width: 0"
                    value="Your wallet: the coin balance, the bonus balance and your VIP pass."/>
            </hstack>
            <hstack class="killRow">
              <image icon="minus" iconSize="12" color="rgba(255,255,255,0.4)" a11yHidden="true"/>
              <text class="killTxt" style="flex: 1; min-width: 0"
                    value="Every episode you unlocked, your transaction history and your orders."/>
            </hstack>
            <hstack class="killRow">
              <image icon="minus" iconSize="12" color="rgba(255,255,255,0.4)" a11yHidden="true"/>
              <text class="killTxt" style="flex: 1; min-width: 0"
                    value="Watch progress, My List, check-ins, spins, task claims and ad views."/>
            </hstack>
            <hstack class="killRow">
              <image icon="minus" iconSize="12" color="rgba(255,255,255,0.4)" a11yHidden="true"/>
              <text class="killTxt" style="flex: 1; min-width: 0"
                    value="Every comment you posted, everyone you blocked, every report you filed, and the account record itself."/>
            </hstack>
          </vstack>
        </vstack>

        <!-- THE COIN BALANCE IS NAMED, and it is not a reason to refuse. Apple is explicit
             that an unspent balance does not license keeping the account; the honest thing
             is to say what happens to it BEFORE the tap. -->
        <text class="bridgeBody"
              value="Any unspent coins and any days left on a VIP pass are destroyed with the account. They cannot be transferred to another account and they are not refunded. A purchase is refunded only by whoever took the payment — the App Store, Google Play, or Stripe on the web — so ask for a refund there BEFORE you delete, because afterwards there is no order history left to point at."/>

        <!-- not wired: the gap, named in place, with the exact thing that closes it -->
        <vstack class="bridge" visible-if="dsx.variable.canDelete == false && global.auth.ok == true">
          <text value="Deletion is not wired in this deployment" class="bridgeTitle"/>
          <text class="bridgeBody" value="Erasing the account touches ten owner-scoped entities behind Postgres RLS, so it is one declared server action — there is no client route that can reach nine of them. Declare `deleteAccount` in `server/viewer.dsx`, route it at POST /viewer/delete, and set `accountDeleteUrl` in App.json `consts`. docs/auth.md has the exact shape. Nothing here pretends to delete in the meantime."/>
        </vstack>

        <vstack spacing="12" alignItems="stretch" visible-if="dsx.variable.canDelete">
          <vstack spacing="6" alignItems="stretch">
            <text value="Type DELETE to confirm" class="fieldCap"/>
            <hstack class="field">
              <image icon="trash" iconSize="16" color="rgba(255,255,255,0.4)" a11yHidden="true"/>
              <textfield bind="dsx.variable.confirmWord" placeholder="DELETE" color="#FFFFFF"
                         style="flex: 1" a11yLabel="Type the word DELETE to confirm"/>
            </hstack>
          </vstack>
          <!-- The confirm gate is the BUTTON'S EXISTENCE, not a greyed-out one: there is no
               `disabled` in the element grammar, and a button with no handler announces to a
               screen reader as a control that does nothing. -->
          <text class="legal" visible-if="dsx.variable.confirmed == false"
                value="Type DELETE above and the button appears. Nothing is sent until you press it."/>
          <button label="{{ dsx.variable.busy ? 'Deleting…' : 'Delete my account permanently' }}"
                  class="danger" role="destructive" visible-if="dsx.variable.confirmed"
                  on:tap="dsx.action.deleteAccount()"/>
        </vstack>

        <vstack class="bridge" visible-if="dsx.variable.deleteState == 'absent'">
          <text value="This deployment answered 404" class="bridgeTitle"/>
          <text class="bridgeBody" value="Nothing was deleted. The route named by `accountDeleteUrl` does not exist on this origin — declare `deleteAccount` in `server/viewer.dsx` and route it. docs/auth.md, 'Account deletion'."/>
        </vstack>
        <hstack class="noticeRow" visible-if="dsx.variable.deleteState == 'refused'">
          <image icon="exclamationmark.triangle.fill" iconSize="14" color="{{ gold() }}" a11yHidden="true"/>
          <text value="{{ dsx.variable.deleteNote }}" class="noticeLine" style="flex: 1; min-width: 0"/>
        </hstack>

        <button label="Keep my account" class="ghost" on:tap="dsx.action.finish({ what: 'cancel' })"/>
      </vstack>
    </vstack>
  </vstack>
</vstack>
